&lt;p&gt;Teddy orders two eggs and a latte. Brulee logs breakfast. Then Teddy asks the question that made this worth showing on screen: “How much protein did I get?”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/3GBcnRemfbI" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Brulee and Teddy are two of the eight Live Voice mascots in &lt;a href="https://www.fud-ai.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Fud AI&lt;/a&gt;. For this short, I wanted the mascots to introduce the feature without asking viewers to take a cartoon’s word for what the app did.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Why the cut to the diary matters
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A spoken “logged” is easy to miss. In the iPhone recording you can see which foods landed in the diary, then read the same protein total Brulee gives Teddy: &lt;strong&gt;20 grams&lt;/strong&gt; for this sample breakfast. That gives the follow-up question a purpose: it connects the conversation to something you can inspect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The café exchange is scripted and voiced with ElevenLabs. The screen recording uses sample data, and the dialogue is captioned so the demo still makes sense on mute. If you’re making a demo of a voice interface, try watching yours with the sound off: can you still tell what changed?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/apoorvdarshan/fud-ai" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Fud AI source on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This week’s announcements point to four questions engineering teams can test directly: what does model access cost, what can an agent do inside a workflow, what must an enterprise integration connect, and how can hardware procurement change?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Model access: throughput and quota need workload tests
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zhipu launched GLM-5.4, with one report describing peak output at 70–80 tokens per second&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTFBCOVg1MHVsUm8tUWJ3MzJxN0d3NUlueF9HZG4xSnJkS3d3VDgwVFpTRVJ5NlY2Uy1rTHRYVFYtdjBxT1VKaW1HcGY3X1B6NGp1S1Z2MGdLV3BkX0pka1gwYTRfWF9CLW1YR1FKUENMdGNZeHpVelE?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Zhipu Launches GLM-5.4 Flagship Model, Outputs 70-80 Tokens/s During Peak Hours - BigGo Finance&lt;/a&gt;. The model was also made available through a Coding Plan that added visual understanding and increased weekly quota by 50%&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTFA1cHhLYXBzd2hzRFRtRVBvUTdDV0xRMEpEWHB4aWRqSlg4d3ZNNmpuZGVzaEZObDMtV3dwWTA3ZG95NUtpejE3d2NNeVJPbkFUZmRsVlJyeGZuaGVHcmxESEdCaGE5QUZrd3p6bmw2aklNVG1XQ1E?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Zhipu's GLM-5.4 Launches on Coding Plan, Adds Visual Understanding and Raises Weekly Quota by 50% - BigGo Finance&lt;/a&gt;. Separate coverage describes a looped-transformer architecture and reports that the launch skipped conventional AI benchmarks&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikAFBVV95cUxPcGdzbW9iLWFRYmJhYnRoS2VuemdVaC1McWgtaXozMWVEYndVOVFqbHZrVTJGTEQyMVBjY2xMT2doTm5QVWFBVk9wZkQwRlpTeDkwN0JqOFF1ejc5bFd6aERGLUxKc1RxTm43WWtRZ3BiY3NNVnNyc09NcmhheTBxQWZHSmFUYjUyR28xVDFYY1k?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Z.ai Launches GLM-5.4, Its First Loop Transformer - Technology Org&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Z.ai Debuts GLM-5.4 With a Novel Looped Transformer Architecture, Skipping AI Benchmarks Entirely - Wccftech&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reported throughput, quota increase, and visual input path make GLM-5.4 a practical candidate for workload testing. They do not establish how the model performs on a team’s repositories, how it behaves under concurrent use, or whether it can produce dependable edits in that team’s context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The useful comparison is therefore GLM-5.4 against the team’s current workflow: the existing model, available quota, response time, and supported input types. Tests should use representative repositories and known failure cases. Benchmark claims would add context, but the launch coverage reports that conventional benchmarks were skipped&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikAFBVV95cUxPcGdzbW9iLWFRYmJhYnRoS2VuemdVaC1McWgtaXozMWVEYndVOVFqbHZrVTJGTEQyMVBjY2xMT2doTm5QVWFBVk9wZkQwRlpTeDkwN0JqOFF1ejc5bFd6aERGLUxKc1RxTm43WWtRZ3BiY3NNVnNyc09NcmhheTBxQWZHSmFUYjUyR28xVDFYY1k?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Z.ai Launches GLM-5.4, Its First Loop Transformer - Technology Org&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Z.ai Debuts GLM-5.4 With a Novel Looped Transformer Architecture, Skipping AI Benchmarks Entirely - Wccftech&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Claude Ship brings code agents into pull requests
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anthropic launched Claude Ship for GitHub pull-request review and fixes, with reporting that it lets agents review, fix, and approve code changes&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Anthropic Launches Claude Ship for GitHub Pull Request Review and Fixes - MarkTechPost&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuwFBVV95cUxPOUdkSUFqUUdSaDIwclBOdjhNS2ltR3hsOW4tQTlMTzAzVjVrazZLN3J1eWJsYm02NDBOcUFlLXdoRV9CY3F2OHdwb215cm5mamdRZTBBS19keDhieXZiQ080SnJlTFVsNEpjNkEzMlBjOUhTSHNHTzZwNHJiYTBzSnpDemUwdFNxTXNDRHB3VEs5cnhIUTJpZ1FMd3Z1OTBXRG00Q0dlSmlqUW53eEd2dDJKaVJlRDVQdXFB?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Anthropic launches Claude Ship, letting AI agents review, fix, and approve code changes - TechCrunch&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That placement changes what needs to be evaluated. An agent operating inside a pull-request workflow should be tested through its permissions, proposed changes, approval behavior, and interaction with the team’s existing review process. The cited headlines do not establish what safeguards accompany those actions, so they should not be assumed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The relevant baseline is not autocomplete. It is the current pull-request pipeline. A useful comparison must include review time, the number of proposed changes requiring correction, and whether the team can reliably inspect or reject an agent’s work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Enterprise AI is being packaged around workflows
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Waystar integrated into Google Cloud’s Gemini for healthcare, while Oracle introduced AI agents for clinical trials&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixgFBVV95cUxOS2NJV3VkUW8zTC1tY3ZUQ1RxMUZMUEtJNjd1WUtXbWlRdkxPNGRKQVNEdm9RMzl5T2JZOXB4MTY0OC1BNFo5LXpVeHRrd2IwTExya1pweVZ6QWtmb2FJd3Z4UWRFdVdmcG9mdDMxYk1Zdng1eEpaRC0tVkpJSkhGa0lScWg3SXhxTF92cHZwYlk3cms2NmFSc2Vmd0tMaTYxSGRoM3o1a0FBU1ZPclVMZmVuMEh3QXBkMlI2ZUN6cGtHMWNnanc?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Weekly Rundown: Waystar integrates into Google Cloud’s Gemini for healthcare; Oracle debuts AI agents for clinical trials - Fierce Healthcare&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The announcements show enterprise AI being delivered through domain-specific workflows. They do not provide implementation details or deployment results. For healthcare, that leaves practical questions about data access, permissions, auditability, and incomplete or conflicting records unanswered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Integration depth is a useful adoption filter. Technical decision-makers should ask how many systems an implementation must connect, where human approval enters the workflow, and what happens when an upstream record is missing or contradictory. Those questions are more actionable than the presence of an agent interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Hardware access is a procurement constraint
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reuters reported that China gained access to Nvidia AI chips despite restrictions intended to prevent it&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMirgFBVV95cUxPU05FY1hvQmJod19oM3p3aTZQaS1CdktCWEZsSnk0TmVWREE1a0tmVW5kVnBxdG5VNU1TX2FmWnZ1aTc1UEQyblFkSklCZ3NlcU1XQUZQVDUwTXZ6R3BoVjlRb1RPY0pTNGdmUUdNZkw0Zi1oanJOcXFieGlBdzRTN05pa3FsaVhUWVk0UWpzc0hkN2kySGF6bGRpU1Y1a2IyNi1iaUFQZThLNVZON0E?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;How China got access to Nvidia's AI chips despite rules to stop them - Reuters&lt;/a&gt;. Nvidia sellers were seeking new buyers as U.S. curbs tightened access to China&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Nvidia sellers seek new buyers as U.S. curbs China AI chip access, sources tell CNBC - CNBC&lt;/a&gt;. Buyers were also reported to have been told to rule out Chinese renters when purchasing GPUs&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Two Californian Nvidia AI chip customers are really Chinese-owned — companies used as workaround for government export controls - Tom's Hardware&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Nvidia chip buyers told to rule out Chinese renters to purchase GPUs, sources say - CNBC&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For teams buying hardware in affected markets, these reports turn export controls into a procurement consideration. Availability may depend on the buyer, the seller, and the stated rental restrictions. Engineering and procurement teams therefore need to confirm which rules apply to each planned purchase rather than treating access to a product as the end of the compliance review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;UBS projected that Chinese AI chip output could increase nearly sixfold by 2030 as Nvidia difficulties mount&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Chinese AI chips to jump nearly sixfold by 2030, UBS says, as Nvidia woes mount - South China Morning Post&lt;/a&gt;. That is a forecast, not a delivered result. Its value here is as a planning scenario: cross-border teams may need to assess how different hardware and software choices would affect their workloads if procurement options change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Alibaba used its own chips to train its next-generation Qwen model, with reporting also describing an eye toward a 2027 shift&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimwFBVV95cUxNZ2hBSEZVclQwb0hIYXFPNG9Ta0JLd00wUHhVSXhYUG9jQkxjb1h6SnFnWXZFUXRMaVdsN0g4UmVlcG9wVU1ZbjZhOGt1QmdiWkRqd3hrYkZRYTZhdFBpbkxhYnBoaDI1SERVUjdibHZOTkl5cGROaHNtVTJXVEdTT1VnanhBdU9nekV0RDQwNnRSYm5paGl1U0xHMA?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Alibaba uses its own chips to train next-generation Qwen model - TechNode&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikAFBVV95cUxQUm9hTGFpeHdXS1gwU2NrSGItT1BYNUJSZ0dMS1hmSjMtQVBoTE9NamdmZkRIVjNvamRkMllja21pSWZhQklITlJjMHlISFdpSDFiaVphYjVrS0NyZ0ozdG8zcGRGQmktS2Q2dy1DNnowaVRtYWZYdkp5d1JTSW1yQ0NvOFJVT2c2UXc3cElVZmk?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Alibaba Trains Next Qwen on Own Chips, Eyes 2027 Shift - shattered.io&lt;/a&gt;. The headline fact is architectural diversification in model training. It does not establish that proprietary silicon removes dependence on Nvidia hardware or predict whether the reported shift will occur.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Evaluation and specialized tools become available
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google DeepMind released the open gBench evaluation framework for LLMs at no charge&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTE9LLU9IRXVDeDNSNjBsdzhMWjhsWnlQWDcyaTZxdkwyQUY1akxqQWE1bTZpcU50U01XUTJHSy1IRldRM0ZBYkM5TW0zYjVpUmwxakRtVWgxeVdJSFdlVDI3bU5IUnYtcnpsVURIcjRUSmxlbF9xaFE?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google DeepMind Releases "gBench" Evaluation Framework for Open LLMs Free of Charge - BigGo Finance&lt;/a&gt;. It also presented Mass Spec Skills for identifying molecules in Google Antigravity&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihgFBVV95cUxPV3EzakZvZ2lKbGk1aU5fb19xdGFSeXJfcHFsaG1mYXZtVmhCOUFCVzhwMEF0YzIyLVFaQVYxQWFybXFxeENKdlNRUXFKV1hHRG9xOEtZSnlzN2JHeUFOVEhaYXFVSFpSVEVNeUhNbHJDaVNTbHg3aGp0UDczSTBUV0p1VndLZw?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;From spectrum to structure: Identifying molecules with Mass Spec Skills for Google Antigravity - Google DeepMind&lt;/a&gt;. Gemini 4 Carbon became available in Google Antigravity for AI Pro users&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMib0FVX3lxTE82OGFkWXdyZHZITy1wbXdJTTJvdTFxX2xkMUdlTWtFeWZBSjNwUW54ZXAwcUhsYWJPYlZfbUZzOWdlWGEtZnpyRTROOGdPR2tJcVZ1b083c3FMVW9zRFk0T3Q4ekt3VnpRRU50bHdqNA?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gemini 4 Carbon is now available in Google Antigravity for AI Pro - 9to5Google&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Free access to an evaluation framework can make model comparison easier to reproduce. Its usefulness still depends on whether its tasks represent the organization’s own workloads and failure cases. An open framework supplies a testing method; it does not make the test results representative by itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same distinction applies to Mass Spec Skills. Availability shows that specialized scientific workflows are being added, but teams still need to determine whether the identification process performs as intended on relevant samples and under appropriate expert review&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihgFBVV95cUxPV3EzakZvZ2lKbGk1aU5fb19xdGFSeXJfcHFsaG1mYXZtVmhCOUFCVzhwMEF0YzIyLVFaQVYxQWFybXFxeENKdlNRUXFKV1hHRG9xOEtZSnlzN2JHeUFOVEhaYXFVSFpSVEVNeUhNbHJDaVNTbHg3aGp0UDczSTBUV0p1VndLZw?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;From spectrum to structure: Identifying molecules with Mass Spec Skills for Google Antigravity - Google DeepMind&lt;/a&gt;. Gemini 4 Carbon’s availability similarly establishes access, not a validated production result&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMib0FVX3lxTE82OGFkWXdyZHZITy1wbXdJTTJvdTFxX2xkMUdlTWtFeWZBSjNwUW54ZXAwcUhsYWJPYlZfbUZzOWdlWGEtZnpyRTROOGdPR2tJcVZ1b083c3FMVW9zRFk0T3Q4ekt3VnpRRU50bHdqNA?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gemini 4 Carbon is now available in Google Antigravity for AI Pro - 9to5Google&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The weekly engineering checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The announcements can be separated into three levels:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Announced:&lt;/strong&gt; GLM-5.4, Claude Ship, the Waystar and Oracle healthcare initiatives, and the new Google tooling&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTFA1cHhLYXBzd2hzRFRtRVBvUTdDV0xRMEpEWHB4aWRqSlg4d3ZNNmpuZGVzaEZObDMtV3dwWTA3ZG95NUtpejE3d2NNeVJPbkFUZmRsVlJyeGZuaGVHcmxESEdCaGE5QUZrd3p6bmw2aklNVG1XQ1E?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Zhipu's GLM-5.4 Launches on Coding Plan, Adds Visual Understanding and Raises Weekly Quota by 50% - BigGo Finance&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Anthropic Launches Claude Ship for GitHub Pull Request Review and Fixes - MarkTechPost&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuwFBVV95cUxPOUdkSUFqUUdSaDIwclBOdjhNS2ltR3hsOW4tQTlMTzAzVjVrazZLN3J1eWJsYm02NDBOcUFlLXdoRV9CY3F2OHdwb215cm5mamdRZTBBS19keDhieXZiQ080SnJlTFVsNEpjNkEzMlBjOUhTSHNHTzZwNHJiYTBzSnpDemUwdFNxTXNDRHB3VEs5cnhIUTJpZ1FMd3Z1OTBXRG00Q0dlSmlqUW53eEd2dDJKaVJlRDVQdXFB?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Anthropic launches Claude Ship, letting AI agents review, fix, and approve code changes - TechCrunch&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixgFBVV95cUxOS2NJV3VkUW8zTC1tY3ZUQ1RxMUZMUEtJNjd1WUtXbWlRdkxPNGRKQVNEdm9RMzl5T2JZOXB4MTY0OC1BNFo5LXpVeHRrd2IwTExya1pweVZ6QWtmb2FJd3Z4UWRFdVdmcG9mdDMxYk1Zdng1eEpaRC0tVkpJSkhGa0lScWg3SXhxTF92cHZwYlk3cms2NmFSc2Vmd0tMaTYxSGRoM3o1a0FBU1ZPclVMZmVuMEh3QXBkMlI2ZUN6cGtHMWNnanc?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Weekly Rundown: Waystar integrates into Google Cloud’s Gemini for healthcare; Oracle debuts AI agents for clinical trials - Fierce Healthcare&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihgFBVV95cUxPV3EzakZvZ2lKbGk1aU5fb19xdGFSeXJfcHFsaG1mYXZtVmhCOUFCVzhwMEF0YzIyLVFaQVYxQWFybXFxeENKdlNRUXFKV1hHRG9xOEtZSnlzN2JHeUFOVEhaYXFVSFpSVEVNeUhNbHJDaVNTbHg3aGp0UDczSTBUV0p1VndLZw?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;From spectrum to structure: Identifying molecules with Mass Spec Skills for Google Antigravity - Google DeepMind&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTE9LLU9IRXVDeDNSNjBsdzhMWjhsWnlQWDcyaTZxdkwyQUY1akxqQWE1bTZpcU50U01XUTJHSy1IRldRM0ZBYkM5TW0zYjVpUmwxakRtVWgxeVdJSFdlVDI3bU5IUnYtcnpsVURIcjRUSmxlbF9xaFE?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google DeepMind Releases "gBench" Evaluation Framework for Open LLMs Free of Charge - BigGo Finance&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Available:&lt;/strong&gt; GLM-5.4 entered the Coding Plan, Claude Ship entered the GitHub workflow, Gemini 4 Carbon became available in Google Antigravity, and gBench was released&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTFA1cHhLYXBzd2hzRFRtRVBvUTdDV0xRMEpEWHB4aWRqSlg4d3ZNNmpuZGVzaEZObDMtV3dwWTA3ZG95NUtpejE3d2NNeVJPbkFUZmRsVlJyeGZuaGVHcmxESEdCaGE5QUZrd3p6bmw2aklNVG1XQ1E?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Zhipu's GLM-5.4 Launches on Coding Plan, Adds Visual Understanding and Raises Weekly Quota by 50% - BigGo Finance&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Anthropic Launches Claude Ship for GitHub Pull Request Review and Fixes - MarkTechPost&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMib0FVX3lxTE82OGFkWXdyZHZITy1wbXdJTTJvdTFxX2xkMUdlTWtFeWZBSjNwUW54ZXAwcUhsYWJPYlZfbUZzOWdlWGEtZnpyRTROOGdPR2tJcVZ1b083c3FMVW9zRFk0T3Q4ekt3VnpRRU50bHdqNA?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gemini 4 Carbon is now available in Google Antigravity for AI Pro - 9to5Google&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTE9LLU9IRXVDeDNSNjBsdzhMWjhsWnlQWDcyaTZxdkwyQUY1akxqQWE1bTZpcU50U01XUTJHSy1IRldRM0ZBYkM5TW0zYjVpUmwxakRtVWgxeVdJSFdlVDI3bU5IUnYtcnpsVURIcjRUSmxlbF9xaFE?oc=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google DeepMind Releases "gBench" Evaluation Framework for Open LLMs Free of Charge - BigGo Finance&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Validated for a workload:&lt;/strong&gt; This week’s headlines do not establish that any of these products has met a team’s quality, security, reliability, or cost requirements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That third level is where the announcements become useful. The practical questions are no longer only what launched. They are what each team can measure in its own code, records, evaluation tasks, and procurement routes before committing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A microVM that can push unlimited bytes through its network interface or its disk can make every other VM on the host slow. Firecracker's answer is a rate limiter on each emulated device, built from token buckets. The configuration is a handful of integers, and it is easy to set them to something that does not mean what you think. This post works through what each field does, using Firecracker's &lt;a href="https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/design.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;design document&lt;/a&gt;, its &lt;a href="https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/src/firecracker/swagger/firecracker.yaml" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;API specification&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/api_requests/patch-network-interface.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;network interface update guide&lt;/a&gt;, as of November 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is about Firecracker. It does not describe how any specific platform, Sandscale included, sets its limits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where the limiter sits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Firecracker's VMM thread emulates the VirtIO network and block devices. When the guest sends a packet, the Firecracker I/O thread copies it from the emulated interface to the host TAP device, and the design document says that is where rate limiting is applied. The same goes for block I/O to the backing file. Because it is applied in the VMM, a guest cannot turn it off from inside; it is the host operator's setting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rate limiters exist for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Network interfaces&lt;/strong&gt;, separately for receive (&lt;code&gt;rx_rate_limiter&lt;/code&gt;) and transmit (&lt;code&gt;tx_rate_limiter&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Block devices&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;rate_limiter&lt;/code&gt; on a drive).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The entropy device&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;rate_limiter&lt;/code&gt; on &lt;code&gt;/entropy&lt;/code&gt;), which feeds random bytes to the guest's &lt;code&gt;virtio-rng&lt;/code&gt; driver.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The serial console&lt;/strong&gt;, which the production host guide recommends rate limiting if you enable it at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For vhost-user devices, the design doc says rate limiting is the backend's job, not Firecracker's.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Two buckets per limiter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each limiter has up to two independent token buckets:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;bandwidth&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, where a token is a byte.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;ops&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, where a token is an operation, such as a packet or a disk request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The API spec calls these independent bytes-per-second and operations-per-second limits, and both apply when both are set. A disk doing many tiny writes runs out of &lt;code&gt;ops&lt;/code&gt; first; one doing large sequential reads runs out of &lt;code&gt;bandwidth&lt;/code&gt; first. Configure both if you care about both kinds of noisy neighbour.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Three numbers per bucket
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From the API spec:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: the most tokens the bucket can hold.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;refill_time&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: how many milliseconds it takes to refill the bucket from empty to &lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt;. The refill rate is derived from these two: &lt;code&gt;size / refill_time&lt;/code&gt; tokens per millisecond.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;one_time_burst&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (optional): an extra initial budget. The spec says refilling starts only after the initial burst is used.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once a bucket is empty, the device can go only as fast as the refill rate. While it has tokens, consumption is not limited in speed, only in amount. So &lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; is both "how big a burst after a quiet period" and, with &lt;code&gt;refill_time&lt;/code&gt;, "what sustained rate".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Reading a config
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the example from Firecracker's network update guide:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"tx_rate_limiter"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"bandwidth"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"size"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"one_time_burst"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1048576&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"refill_time"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Read it as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sustained rate: 1024 bytes per 1000 ms, which is 1 KiB a second. That is very low, fine for a documentation example and not for a real workload.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Burst after the bucket fills: at most 1024 bytes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One-off budget at the start: 1 MiB, used first, before refilling begins.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And a more realistic shape for a sandbox that should be able to fetch a package but not saturate a link:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"rx_rate_limiter"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"bandwidth"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"size"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10485760&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"refill_time"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"ops"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"size"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"refill_time"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That is 10 MiB of tokens refilled over 1000 ms, so a sustained 10 MiB a second with bursts up to 10 MiB, and 2000 packets a second. Pick your own numbers from your host's capacity and how many VMs share it; these are here to show the arithmetic, not as a recommendation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Common mistakes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Treating &lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; as a rate.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; is a capacity. The rate is &lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; divided by &lt;code&gt;refill_time&lt;/code&gt;. Doubling &lt;code&gt;refill_time&lt;/code&gt; halves the rate without changing the burst.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Forgetting &lt;code&gt;refill_time&lt;/code&gt; is milliseconds.&lt;/strong&gt; A &lt;code&gt;refill_time&lt;/code&gt; of 1 makes the refill rate &lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; tokens per millisecond, which is a thousand times what you probably meant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Setting only &lt;code&gt;bandwidth&lt;/code&gt; on a disk.&lt;/strong&gt; Small random writes can still generate enough operations to hurt other guests. The production host guide lists rate limiting alongside the jailer's resource limits as the tools for noisy-neighbour storage contention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Assuming the limiter covers everything on the interface.&lt;/strong&gt; Frames the guest sends to Firecracker's metadata service (MMDS) are answered inside Firecracker and never reach the TAP device, so the MMDS design doc says they are not charged to the TX limiter. It recommends a cgroup CPU quota to bound the CPU spent answering them. See &lt;a href="https://sandscale.dev/blog/firecracker-mmds-explained" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the MMDS post&lt;/a&gt; for more.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Changing limits on a running VM
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Network limiters can be changed after boot with &lt;code&gt;PATCH /network-interfaces/{id}&lt;/code&gt;, and block drives can be patched with &lt;code&gt;PATCH /drives/{id}&lt;/code&gt;. The update is merged with the existing settings: patching &lt;code&gt;rx_rate_limiter&lt;/code&gt; leaves &lt;code&gt;tx_rate_limiter&lt;/code&gt; alone. To remove a limit, set a bucket with &lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; 0 and &lt;code&gt;refill_time&lt;/code&gt; 0.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That makes dynamic policies possible. A host agent can give a VM a generous limit while it installs dependencies, then tighten it while untrusted code runs, without restarting anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What rate limiting is not
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rate limiting controls how much and how fast. It does not control where. A guest limited to a trickle can still send that trickle to any address the host routes. Firecracker's design doc says it does no network traffic filtering at all and that guest egress should be filtered at the host. Destination control is a firewall or proxy's job; see &lt;a href="https://sandscale.dev/blog/egress-allowlist-design-for-agent-sandboxes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;designing an egress allowlist&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is also not a CPU or memory limit. Those come from cgroups, which the jailer sets up, and from the VM's configured vCPUs and memory. &lt;a href="https://sandscale.dev/blog/resource-exhaustion-in-agent-sandboxes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Resource exhaustion in agent sandboxes&lt;/a&gt; covers the guest-side view of those limits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where Sandscale fits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sandscale sandboxes are Firecracker microVMs, and on Sandscale you do not set token buckets yourself. What you choose is the network mode, &lt;code&gt;none&lt;/code&gt; by default or &lt;code&gt;allowlist&lt;/code&gt; where your plan includes it, the size of the box within your plan's limits, and its time limit. The &lt;a href="https://sandscale.dev/docs/reference/plans" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;plans reference&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://sandscale.dev/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt; list what each plan allows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on the &lt;a href="https://sandscale.dev/blog/firecracker-rate-limiters-explained" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sandscale blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most apps don't do everything inside a single request. Sending an email, processing an upload, generating a report, syncing with another service, crunching a big dataset — all of that takes time. If you try to do it all inside an HTTP request, responses get slow, servers get overloaded, and things start breaking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why we move slow work into the background.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But here's the catch: moving work to the background doesn't automatically make it reliable. Workers crash. Networks time out. External services go down. And sometimes a task runs twice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So if you're building background jobs in Python, you have to plan for failure from the start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this article, I'll walk through how to design background jobs that actually hold up — how to retry sensibly, how to stop duplicate work with idempotency, and how to recover when things go wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;1. Understanding Background Processing&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Background processing just means doing work outside the user's immediate request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Say your app lets users generate PDF reports. The naive way looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/generate-report"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;generate_report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;report&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;generate_large_pdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"report"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That works until report generation takes 30 seconds, or two minutes. The user is left waiting. And if the request times out, it fails even though the work was still happening.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A better approach is to split submission from execution. You accept the request, create a job, return a task ID, and let a worker handle the heavy lifting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It usually goes like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Client submits a request&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;2. App validates it&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3. App creates a background task&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;4. Task goes into a queue&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;5. A worker picks it up&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;6. Worker runs the operation&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;7. App records success or failure&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;8. Client can check the task status&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You get faster responses, and your API isn't stuck waiting on slow work.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;2. Choosing the Right Background Processing Architecture&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Python gives you a few options. Which one you pick depends on how important the task is and how much infrastructure you want to manage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In-process background tasks&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For simple stuff, you can use what's built into your framework. FastAPI has BackgroundTasks:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fastapi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundTasks&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;send_notification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Sending notification to {email}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/notify"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;notify_user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;background_tasks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BackgroundTasks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;background_tasks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;send_notification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"developer@example.com"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Notification scheduled"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is fine for lightweight, non-critical tasks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But it's not a real job queue. If the process restarts, the task is gone. There's no persistent storage, no real recovery, no distributed workers. For anything important, you want something more durable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Distributed task queues&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A distributed queue separates submitting a job from running it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Common Python options:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Celery:&lt;/strong&gt; The heavyweight option. Multiple workers, brokers, retries, scheduling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;RQ:&lt;/strong&gt; Redis-backed, much simpler to reason about.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Dramatiq:&lt;/strong&gt; Built around actors and middleware, good for message processing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A typical setup looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;         `Client
|
v
FastAPI Backend
|
v
Task Submission
|
v
Message Broker
|
+-----+-----+
|           |
v           v
Worker 1    Worker 2
|           |
+-----+-----+
|
v
External Services
|
v
Result Database`
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Backend submits jobs, broker holds them, workers do the work. You can scale API servers and workers separately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is more infrastructure to monitor and maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;3. Why Background Jobs Fail&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before you fix reliability, you need to know how jobs actually fail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Network failures&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your worker calls an external API. The API is down for a minute, the connection times out, the network blips.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Worker termination&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A worker dies mid-task because of a deploy, an OOM kill, a server restart, or a bug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;External service failures&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A payment provider rejects a request. An email service is having a bad day. A cloud provider throttles you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Duplicate execution&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A worker finishes the work but crashes before acknowledging the message. The broker hands the same message to another worker, and it runs again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Invalid task data&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some failures aren't temporary. The data is bad, the resource doesn't exist, the business rule is violated. Retrying that 10 times won't help.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reliable system has to tell the difference between "try again later" and "this will never work."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;4. Implementing Retry Mechanisms&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retries let a worker try again when the failure is temporary. If an external service hiccups, wait a bit and try again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But retrying immediately is usually a bad idea.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine a service is already overloaded. If hundreds of workers all retry at once, you just make the outage worse. That's a retry storm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Exponential backoff&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is to wait longer after each failure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;delay = min(max_delay, base_delay * 2^attempt)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With a base delay of 2 seconds:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Attempt| Approximate delay
1| 2 seconds
2| 4 seconds
3| 8 seconds
4| 16 seconds
5| 32 seconds
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The max delay stops it growing forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Adding jitter&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Jitter adds randomness so workers don't all retry at the exact same moment.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;calculate_retry_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;base_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;2.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;60.0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;exponential_delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;base_delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uniform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;exponential_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This picks a random delay between 0 and the exponential limit. It spreads the retry traffic out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Implementing retries with asyncio&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For async Python, you can write a small retry helper:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;asyncio&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;collections&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;abc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Awaitable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Callable&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;typing&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;TypeVar&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="no"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;TypeVar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"T"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;retry_operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Callable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Awaitable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;base_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;2.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;60.0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;except&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TimeoutError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ConnectionError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max_attempts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;exponential_delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;base_delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uniform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;exponential_delay&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;asyncio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RuntimeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Operation failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;usage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;httpx&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch_external_data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;httpx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;AsyncClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;10.0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://api.example.com/data"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;retry_operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;fetch_external_data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;print(result)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In production, you still have to decide:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;- Which HTTP status codes are worth retrying?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;- Should you retry auth failures? Usually no&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;- What about rate limits?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;- Does the service send a Retry-After header?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;- Is this operation even safe to repeat?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Don't retry everything. Retry with intent.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;5. Understanding Idempotency&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Retries create a new problem: duplicate execution&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Say a background task creates an invoice. The worker creates it, but then loses connection before acknowledging the message. The broker redelivers it. Without protection, you now have two invoices for one request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;That's where idempotency comes in&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An operation is idempotent if running it again with the same input gives you the same result as running it once — no duplicate side effects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Using idempotency keys&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An idempotency key is a unique ID for one logical operation.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;uuid&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;create_idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;uuid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uuid4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You generate the key when the operation is first submitted, and you reuse that same key for every retry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A simple table might look like this:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;background_jobs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;UUID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;PRIMARY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;VARCHAR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;255&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;UNIQUE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;VARCHAR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;JSONB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;created_at&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;TIMESTAMPTZ&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;DEFAULT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;NOW&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The unique constraint stops you creating two job records with the same key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But that's only half of it. You also have to make sure the business operation itself can't happen twice:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;process_invoice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;invoice_data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;existing&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;find_invoice_by_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;existing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;existing&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;create_invoice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;invoice_data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Those functions are placeholders — you'd implement them with proper transaction handling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For real protection, enforce the key with a database unique constraint, and make the lookup + creation atomic. That matters a lot when multiple workers could process related messages at the same time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Idempotency is not exactly-once execution&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an important distinction. Most distributed systems give you at-least-once delivery. A task might arrive more than once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of pretending it will run exactly once, design it so running twice doesn't cause duplicate business effects. That's the practical way to think about reliability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;6. Managing Task States&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your system should keep a clear record of where each task is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A basic model:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;State| Meaning&lt;br&gt;
Pending| Task has been submitted but not started&lt;br&gt;
Running| A worker is processing the task&lt;br&gt;
Completed| Processing finished successfully&lt;br&gt;
Failed| Processing stopped after an unrecoverable error&lt;br&gt;
Retrying| Another attempt is scheduled&lt;br&gt;
Cancelled| Task was cancelled before completion&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That makes monitoring and debugging so much easier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For example:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;enum&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Enum&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pydantic&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;JobStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Enum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="no"&gt;PENDING&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"pending"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="no"&gt;RUNNING&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"running"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="no"&gt;RETRYING&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"retrying"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="no"&gt;COMPLETED&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"completed"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="no"&gt;FAILED&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"failed"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="no"&gt;CANCELLED&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cancelled"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;JobRecord&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;job_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;JobStatus&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;error_message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;created_at&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;updated_at&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is just a data model. In production, you'd store it in durable storage and be careful about how state transitions happen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;7. Recovering from Failed Jobs&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retries help with temporary failures, but they shouldn't run forever. You need a clear plan for when a job just won't succeed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Maximum retry limits&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every task needs a retry limit. Once you hit it, stop retrying automatically and record the final failure. Otherwise permanently failing jobs will eat your resources forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dead-letter queues&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A dead-letter queue holds messages that failed even after retries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of vanishing silently, failed jobs go somewhere you can actually look at them. You can check the error, figure out what went wrong, and decide if it should be fixed and resubmitted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Failure classification&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It helps to split errors into two buckets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Transient failures:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Temporary network interruptions&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Service unavailability&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Request timeouts.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Temporary rate limiting.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Permanent failures:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Invalid input.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Missing required resources.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Unsupported operations.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Auth errors that need human intervention.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retry the transient ones. Record and surface the permanent ones — don't keep hammering them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Graceful worker shutdown&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Workers need to handle shutdowns well too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During a deploy, a worker might get a termination signal mid-task. Ideally it either finishes within a grace period, or the task can be safely redelivered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Exactly how you do that depends on your queue, your ack settings, and your worker setup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;8. Observability: Knowing What Your Workers Are Doing&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A job that fails silently is a nightmare to maintain. You need to know what happened, when, and why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;At a minimum, log:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Job identifier.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Task type.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Current status.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Attempt count.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Execution duration.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Failure reason.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Worker identifier.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Submission and completion timestamps.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Python's logging module is a good start:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;logging&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;logging&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getLogger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute_job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;job_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;info&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Starting background job"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;extra&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"job_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"running"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;process_job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;job_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;info&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Background job completed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;extra&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"job_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"completed"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;except&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Exception&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;exception&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Background job failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;extra&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"job_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"failed"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here process_job() is assumed to be implemented elsewhere.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In production, send those structured logs to a central platform. And track metrics like:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Queue length.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Average task duration.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Job failure rate.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Retry frequency.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Worker availability.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Jobs waiting too long.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those numbers will tell you something is wrong before your users do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;9. Testing Background Job Reliability&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing background jobs isn't just "does it return the right result?" You also need to test what happens when things break.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Good scenarios to cover:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Successful execution:&lt;/strong&gt; Task completes and records the expected result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Temporary failure:&lt;/strong&gt; First attempt fails, later attempt succeeds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Maximum retry reached:&lt;/strong&gt; Task stops after the configured limit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Duplicate delivery:&lt;/strong&gt; Same task processed twice, but no duplicate business effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Worker interruption:&lt;/strong&gt; Processing is interrupted and the task can be safely recovered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Invalid input:&lt;/strong&gt; Task is rejected or marked as permanently failed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concurrent execution:&lt;/strong&gt; Multiple workers process related tasks without corrupting state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Here's a unit test for the retry helper:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pytest&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@pytest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;asyncio&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;test_retry_operation_succeeds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;unstable_operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;nonlocal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ConnectionError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Temporary connection failure"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"success"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;retry_operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;unstable_operation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;base_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.001&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.001&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"success"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That confirms the operation is retried and eventually succeeds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You should also test that permanent errors aren't retried, and that the max attempt limit is actually respected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;10. Production Design Checklist&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before you ship a background-processing system, run through this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Use a durable queue when task persistence is required.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Define clear task states.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Set connection and execution timeouts.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Implement bounded retry policies.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Use exponential backoff and jitter where appropriate.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Classify transient and permanent failures.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Implement idempotency for operations that may be repeated.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Use database constraints to prevent duplicate business records.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Configure dead-letter handling.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Implement graceful worker shutdown.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Monitor queue length and worker health.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Record structured logs and execution metrics.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Test duplicate delivery and failure recovery.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;[ ] Protect sensitive information in task payloads and logs.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Get those right and your system will be a lot easier to run and debug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Background jobs are a core part of modern Python apps, but moving work out of an HTTP request doesn't make failure go away.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reliability takes deliberate work.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retries help you recover from temporary failures. Exponential backoff and jitter keep you from hammering a struggling service. Idempotency stops duplicate work from creating duplicate business effects. Task states, dead-letter queues, logging, and monitoring make failures easier to find and fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And most importantly: design for the case where a task gets interrupted, delivered twice, or fails in a weird place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reliable system doesn't assume everything works the first time. It's built to handle failure, recover safely, and keep your data correct.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the difference between just running background tasks and actually engineering a reliable background-processing system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Author:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ezenwa Christian Chimeremeze.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Focus:&lt;/strong&gt; Technical Writing | Developer Documentation | Python | Backend Engineering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


CodeNewbie Podcast
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted an ID badge that hangs from a strap, swings when you grab it and flies when you throw it. Every example I found pulled in a physics engine and a 3D renderer for it. That is a lot of code to ship for one card.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It turns out the whole thing is a rope, a weight and one spring. Here is how it works. You can &lt;a href="https://goodui.dev/components/pro/lanyard-badge" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;try the finished badge here&lt;/a&gt; first: grab it and throw it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. The strap is 14 links
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A rope is a chain of points that are not allowed to drift apart. I store each point with its current and its previous position:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Pt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;py&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There is no velocity field. The velocity &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt; the difference between the two positions. This is Verlet integration, and it is why throwing works without any extra code: when you let go, the last two positions already contain the speed of your hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each step moves every point by its own velocity, plus gravity:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;vx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.99&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// a little air drag&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;vy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;py&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.99&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;px&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;py&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;vx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;vy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;GRAVITY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dt&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


2. Then pull the chain back together
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After that step the points are too far apart. So for each neighbouring pair, measure the distance and move both points toward the right length. Repeat a few times:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dist&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;hypot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pull&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dist&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;segment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dist&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pull&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wa&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pull&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wa&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pull&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wb&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pull&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wb&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;wa&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;wb&lt;/code&gt; decide who gives way. Normally both are &lt;code&gt;0.5&lt;/code&gt;. Three cases are different:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The top point is fixed, so it never moves (&lt;code&gt;wa = 0&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The card is heavy, so on the last link the rope does most of the moving (&lt;code&gt;0.88&lt;/code&gt; against &lt;code&gt;0.12&lt;/code&gt;). This one number is what makes it hang straight and swing like a pendulum instead of flapping like a ribbon.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;While you hold the card, it does not give at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ten passes per step are enough for 14 links.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. The card over-swings on its own spring
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the card simply took the angle of the last link, it would look glued to the rope. A real badge lags behind and overshoots. So the card has its own angle and its own angular speed, pulled toward the rope's direction by a spring:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;want&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;atan2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;spin&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;want&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;angle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;90&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spin&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dt&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;angle&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spin&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dt&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The card also leans in 3D with its horizontal speed (&lt;code&gt;rotateY&lt;/code&gt;), and a band of light moves across it by the same amount. Both are one line each and they sell the effect more than anything else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Dragging, and the two bugs I hit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While dragging, the last point follows the pointer. Two things went wrong at first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The rope stretched forever.&lt;/strong&gt; The top is fixed and so is your hand, so the constraints cannot be satisfied. The fix is to limit the hand: past the strap's length the card only follows 12% of the extra distance. It feels like the strap going taut.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A fast flick launched the card into orbit.&lt;/strong&gt; Verlet keeps whatever speed the last two positions imply, and a mouse can move very fast between two frames. I cap the release speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Do not run when nothing moves
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A physics loop that runs forever drains batteries. This one counts frames in which the card is slow, the spin is near zero and nobody is holding it. After half a second of that it stops, and a pointer event starts it again. It also stops off screen (IntersectionObserver) and in a hidden tab.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt; the simulation never starts: the badge hangs straight and still flips on click.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it costs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;About 500 lines in one file, styles and markup included, with an SVG path for the strap and a &lt;code&gt;div&lt;/code&gt; for the card. The physics uses no library at all. The only import besides React is one hook from Motion that reads the reduced-motion setting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The finished component, with a flip side and keyboard control, is part of &lt;a href="https://goodui.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;goodui&lt;/a&gt;, a React component library I am building. 133 of the components are free and MIT licensed, installed as source through the shadcn CLI. The lanyard badge is in the paid set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Is scroll‑state() and Why It Matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CSS scroll‑state() is a media query‑like feature that lets you apply styles based on the current scrolling activity of an element or the viewport. Unlike the traditional &lt;code&gt;:hover&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;:focus&lt;/code&gt; pseudo‑classes, scroll‑state() can detect whether the user is actively scrolling, has stopped, or has scrolled to the top or bottom. This opens up new UI possibilities such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Showing a “Back to top” button only after the user has scrolled past a threshold.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Changing a header’s background color when the page is being scrolled.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Triggering lazy‑load animations when the user stops scrolling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the feature is purely CSS, it keeps JavaScript logic minimal and can be leveraged directly in React components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to Write a scroll‑state() Query
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The syntax follows the same pattern as other media queries:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;scrolling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;…&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;…&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;top&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;…&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;bottom&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;…&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You can also combine conditions with &lt;code&gt;and&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;scrolling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;orientation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;portrait&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;…&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;These queries are evaluated against the element they are applied to. When used on &lt;code&gt;:root&lt;/code&gt;, they refer to the viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Using scroll‑state() in a React Component
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let’s create a simple header that changes its background color while scrolling.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Header.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fixed w-full z-10 transition-colors duration-300"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"text-xl p-4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;My&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Site&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Add the CSS that reacts to the scrolling state:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* styles.css */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;scrolling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;background-color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;rgba&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;255&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;255&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;255&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;background-color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;transparent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Because the CSS is global, it automatically applies to the &lt;code&gt;Header&lt;/code&gt; component. No JavaScript is required to detect the scroll event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Integrating with Tailwind CSS
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tailwind does not ship with scroll‑state() utilities out of the box, but you can extend it. Add a custom plugin in &lt;code&gt;tailwind.config.js&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// tailwind.config.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// …&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;addVariant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addVariant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'scroll-state-scrolling'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;modifySelectors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;separator&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;modifySelectors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`.scroll-state-scrolling\\:${e(className)}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now you can use the variant in your JSX:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fixed w-full z-10 transition-colors duration-300
bg-transparent scroll-state-scrolling:bg-white/80"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"text-xl p-4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;My&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Site&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;scroll-state-scrolling:&lt;/code&gt; prefix tells Tailwind to apply the style only when the element is being scrolled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Performance Considerations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Avoid heavy CSS in scroll‑state queries&lt;/strong&gt; – The browser must repaint the element whenever the state changes. Keep styles simple (e.g., background colors, transforms).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Prefer &lt;code&gt;will-change&lt;/code&gt; for animations&lt;/strong&gt; – If you animate properties that depend on scroll state, use &lt;code&gt;will-change&lt;/code&gt; to hint the compositor thread.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;@media (scroll-state: idle)&lt;/code&gt; sparingly&lt;/strong&gt; – Idle state fires after scrolling stops, which can trigger expensive recalculations if you’re running JavaScript in the same event loop.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test on mobile&lt;/strong&gt; – Some mobile browsers may not support scroll‑state() yet. Use &lt;code&gt;@supports&lt;/code&gt; to provide fallbacks:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@supports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;scrolling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* fallback styles */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Combining scroll‑state() with Intersection Observer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For more complex interactions, pair scroll‑state() with the Intersection Observer API. For example, you can trigger a CSS animation only when an element is visible &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; the user is not scrolling:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// In a component&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;classList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'visible'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;classList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;remove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'visible'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;threshold&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;querySelectorAll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'.animate-on-visible'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;observe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then in CSS:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.animate-on-visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;transition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.3s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.animate-on-visible.visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Accessibility Tips
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Avoid flashing content&lt;/strong&gt; – Rapid changes triggered by scroll‑state() can be disorienting. Keep transitions smooth.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Provide keyboard alternatives&lt;/strong&gt; – If you hide elements while scrolling, ensure they remain reachable via keyboard navigation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CSS scroll‑state() offers a clean, declarative way to adapt UI based on scrolling activity. In React, it keeps component logic minimal, and when combined with Tailwind’s utility‑first approach, you can write expressive, maintainable code. Keep performance in mind by limiting complex styles and testing across browsers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Does scroll‑state() work in all modern browsers?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scroll‑state() is supported in recent versions of Chrome, Edge, and Safari. Firefox support is pending. Always check the latest compatibility on caniuse.com.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Can I use scroll‑state() on an element that isn’t scrollable?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. When applied to &lt;code&gt;:root&lt;/code&gt;, it reflects the viewport’s scroll state. For non‑scrollable elements, the query will never match unless the element itself can scroll (e.g., &lt;code&gt;overflow: auto&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. How do I provide a fallback for browsers that don’t support scroll‑state()?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Wrap your CSS in an &lt;code&gt;@supports&lt;/code&gt; rule:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@supports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;scrolling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* fallback styles here */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This ensures graceful degradation for unsupported browsers.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This post was drafted with AI assistance.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Removing Effect Dependencies — Summary
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/removing-effect-dependencies#to-change-the-dependencies-change-the-code" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://react.dev/learn/removing-effect-dependencies#to-change-the-dependencies-change-the-code&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key points of "To change the dependencies, change the code"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dependency array isn't something you choose. It is determined by the Effect's code. If you want different dependencies, change the code, not the array.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The process has three steps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Change the Effect's code, or the way its values are declared.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Follow the linter and update the dependency array to match the new code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If you're not satisfied, go back to step 1.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suppressing the dependency warning with &lt;code&gt;eslint-disable&lt;/code&gt; can cause bugs, because the Effect keeps using stale values.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To remove a dependency, you have to show the linter that the value isn't reactive. For example, if you move &lt;code&gt;roomId&lt;/code&gt; outside the component as a constant, the dependency array can be empty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Recipes from the rest of the page
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Situation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Fix&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You're using an Effect to respond to a specific action, such as clicking a send button&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Move the logic into an event handler&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One Effect does two unrelated things&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Split it into separate Effects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You read state only to compute the next state from the previous one&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use an updater function (&lt;code&gt;msgs =&amp;gt; [...msgs, msg]&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You want to read a value without re-running the Effect when it changes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Extract it into an Effect Event (&lt;code&gt;useEffectEvent&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An object or function is recreated on every render&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Move it outside the component, create it inside the Effect, or extract primitive values from it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Objects and functions are different on every render, even when their contents are identical (&lt;code&gt;Object.is&lt;/code&gt; returns &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;). Using them as dependencies makes the Effect re-run too often. To avoid this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Move static objects and functions outside the component.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create dynamic ones inside the Effect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Extract primitive values such as &lt;code&gt;roomId&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;serverUrl&lt;/code&gt;, and depend on those instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Notes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Treat linter warnings like compile errors.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When you want fewer dependencies, review the code first, not the array.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've ever bought a phone or laptop in Bangladesh, you know the routine: open Star Tech, Ryans, Pickaboo, Apple Gadgets and a few Facebook-page shops in separate tabs, then compare prices by hand. The same model can differ by several thousand taka between shops, and prices change every week.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I got tired of that, so I built &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://komkoto.bd/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;KomKoto.bd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (কম কত — "how much less?"). It shows the listed price of one product across Bangladeshi retailers on a single page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Compares prices across 22 shops.&lt;/strong&gt; Star Tech, Ryans, Pickaboo, Apple Gadgets, KRY International, Techland BD, UCC, Daffodil Computers, Rokomari and more. It currently tracks about 17,000 products.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Groups the same product.&lt;/strong&gt; "iPhone 18 Pro Max 256GB" from four shops shows up as one product, with each shop's price and stock status.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Flags real price drops.&lt;/strong&gt; Today's lowest price is compared with the product's typical price over the last 30 days, so a "sale" has to be below the usual price to count.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sends price alerts.&lt;/strong&gt; Pick a variant and a target price, and you get an email when an in-stock offer reaches it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Works in Bangla and English.&lt;/strong&gt; Category names and the main interface use both.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How it's built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend:&lt;/strong&gt; React + TypeScript + Tailwind, built with Vite.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;API:&lt;/strong&gt; FastAPI serving a read-only public catalog API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Database:&lt;/strong&gt; PostgreSQL.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Search:&lt;/strong&gt; Meilisearch, kept in sync by a background worker.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scrapers:&lt;/strong&gt; one Python scraper per retailer. Some shops expose a WooCommerce Store API, which is much easier to work with than parsing HTML.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The hard part: matching products
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scraping prices is the easy half. Deciding that two listings are the &lt;em&gt;same product&lt;/em&gt; is much harder. Retailers name products very differently:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Apple iPhone 18 Pro Max (256GB) - Cosmic Orange
iPhone 18 Pro Max 256 GB Orange (Official)
iPhone 18 ProMax 12/256GB
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Storage, RAM, colour, region and "official vs. unofficial" all matter for the price. A wrong match is worse than no match, because it shows people a price they can't actually get.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My pipeline combines text normalisation with an embedding-based candidate search, then runs a small locally fine-tuned model to decide whether two listings match. Uncertain cases go to a human review queue. Even after fine-tuning, a blind benchmark still showed some false automatic matches, so I don't let the model run unattended on large batches yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Lessons so far
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Be careful with "lowest price".&lt;/strong&gt; Shops sometimes list prices for out-of-stock items, or prices without VAT or warranty. KomKoto only treats in-stock offers as live, and it reminds people to check the shop's final price.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Variants deserve their own pages.&lt;/strong&gt; An 8/128 phone and a 12/256 phone are different purchases.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Keep admin tools off the server.&lt;/strong&gt; The scrapers, the matcher and the admin panel run on my own machine. The public server only runs the read-only site, the API and search.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're shopping for a phone, laptop, earbuds or PC parts in Bangladesh, try &lt;a href="https://komkoto.bd/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;KomKoto.bd&lt;/a&gt; and tell me which shops or categories are missing. I'm also happy to answer questions about the scraping or matching setup in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For decades, web design has operated under a single, fundamental constraint: anticipation. As designers and developers, we spend months mapping user flows, wireframing edge cases, and building responsive grids. We try to anticipate every possible action, device size, and user intent. The result is a web of templates—polished, functional, but ultimately rigid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Enter &lt;strong&gt;Generative UI&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generative UI is a paradigm shift that turns this model on its head. Instead of forcing users to navigate pre-built layouts, the interface generates itself in real-time, tailored precisely to the user's intent. When a user asks an AI assistant to "compare the price of flights to Tokyo next month and show me a breakdown of hotel costs," they don't get a wall of text or a standard search result page. Instead, the interface dynamically constructs a custom comparison table, a bar chart, and an interactive booking widget—assembled on-the-fly and rendered directly in their browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This blog post will dive deep into how Generative UI works, explore the architectural patterns powering this transformation, outline critical UX principles for designing in the AI era, and walk through a real-world code implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Understanding the Shift: From Chatbots to Generative Interfaces
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To understand Generative UI, we must first look at the evolution of human-computer interaction (HCI) in the era of Artificial Intelligence:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Conversational Interfaces (The Chatbot Era):&lt;/strong&gt; The first wave of consumer AI apps relied heavily on chat. Users entered text, and the AI responded with markdown text. While conversational interfaces are natural, text is a highly inefficient medium for complex tasks. Reading a long itinerary is harder than looking at a timeline; choosing a flight from a text block is slower than using a calendar picker.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Generative UI (The Hybrid Era):&lt;/strong&gt; Generative UI combines the flexibility of natural language processing with the rich interactivity of traditional UI elements. Instead of outputting text, the Large Language Model (LLM) decides &lt;em&gt;which&lt;/em&gt; UI component is best suited to display the requested information, fetches the structured data to populate it, and streams the component onto the screen.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this hybrid paradigm, the LLM is not the interface itself; it is the &lt;strong&gt;orchestrator of the interface&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Core Architecture of Generative UI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How does a server take a natural language prompt and transform it into a live, interactive React or Vue component? The magic lies in a feature called &lt;strong&gt;LLM Tool Calling&lt;/strong&gt; (or Function Calling).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the step-by-step lifecycle of a Generative UI interaction:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt; &lt;strong&gt;User Prompt:&lt;/strong&gt; The user submits a prompt, such as: &lt;em&gt;"Show me the weather in Paris for the next three days."&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Intent Analysis &amp;amp; Tool Selection:&lt;/strong&gt; The LLM processes the query and realizes it needs external data and a visual presentation. It matches the query against a list of registered "tools" (functions) provided by the server. In this case, it selects a tool called &lt;code&gt;getWeather&lt;/code&gt; and infers the arguments: &lt;code&gt;{ city: "Paris", days: 3 }&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Data Fetching:&lt;/strong&gt; The backend executes the helper function associated with the &lt;code&gt;getWeather&lt;/code&gt; tool, fetching live meteorological data from an external API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Structured Mapping:&lt;/strong&gt; The server wraps this retrieved data into a predefined JSON schema.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Component Streaming:&lt;/strong&gt; Instead of just sending raw JSON back to the client, the server maps the tool execution state to a specific React component (e.g., &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;WeatherWidget /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;) and streams the component's render state directly to the client.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Dynamic Rendering:&lt;/strong&gt; The frontend receives the stream, resolves the component, passes the fetched data as props, and renders the interactive UI in real-time with visual transitions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach keeps your application secure and performant. You are not sending arbitrary code from the LLM to the browser (which would be a massive security risk). Instead, you are sending structured data that dynamically populates a trusted, pre-compiled component library residing in your codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


UX Principles for the AI-Driven Canvas
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Designing an interface that changes shape on-the-fly presents unique user experience challenges. Without careful design, Generative UI can feel disorienting, unpredictable, and untrustworthy. Here are the core UX principles for building successful generative experiences:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Visual Stability and Smooth Transitions
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When components are rendering dynamically, layouts can jump unexpectedly, causing visual fatigue or accidental clicks (cumulative layout shift).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Use Skeleton Loaders:&lt;/strong&gt; While the LLM is choosing a tool or fetching data, render a matching skeleton placeholder to reserve the visual space.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Animate Layout Changes:&lt;/strong&gt; Use libraries like Framer Motion or the Web CSS View Transitions API to smoothly animate components scaling, entering, or exiting the canvas.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Graceful Degradation and Fallbacks
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI models are non-deterministic; they can make mistakes. The LLM might pass incomplete arguments to a tool or select the wrong component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Strict Prop Validation:&lt;/strong&gt; Ensure all dynamic components have robust error boundaries and fallback UI states (e.g., if a chart component receives invalid coordinates, fall back to a clean list view).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Text Readback:&lt;/strong&gt; Always provide a plain-text explanation alongside the generated UI. If a component fails to render, the user should still get their answer in text form.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. State Continuity and Two-Way Interactivity
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A generative component shouldn't be a dead-end snapshot. It must be fully interactive and capable of feeding state back into the conversation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Embedded Call-to-Actions:&lt;/strong&gt; If the LLM generates a flight card, clicking "Select flight" should update the global application state and prompt the LLM to generate the next logical step (e.g., hotel selection).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Bi-directional State:&lt;/strong&gt; The AI can modify the component, but the user must also be able to modify the component manually (e.g., adjusting a slider should update the prompt parameters).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Cognitive Load Management
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the UI can render anything, there is a risk of overwhelming the user.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Progressive Disclosure:&lt;/strong&gt; Start with the simplest, most direct component. Let the user expand the widget to see advanced charts or deep-dive details.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  &lt;strong&gt;Scannable Layouts:&lt;/strong&gt; Use strict typography hierarchies, visual groupings, and contrasting colors to help the user instantly register the structure of the dynamically generated component.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Implementation: Building a Generative UI Stream
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's look at how we can implement this pattern using React and the popular Vercel AI SDK. This example demonstrates how to set up an AI agent that renders a custom &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;StockTicker /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;MarketChart /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; component based on user input.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, let's look at the server-side route. This API handler defines the model, handles the incoming prompt, and specifies the tools the model can access:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/api/chat/route.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;openai&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@ai-sdk/openai'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;streamUI&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ai'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'zod'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;StockTicker&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@/components/StockTicker'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MarketChart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@/components/MarketChart'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;streamUI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;openai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'gpt-4o'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;prompt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`&amp;lt;p class="text-gray-700"&amp;gt;${content}&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;showStockPrice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Get the current stock price for a given ticker symbol'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;parameters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;describe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'The stock ticker symbol, e.g., AAPL, MSFT'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;generate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;symbol&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;yield&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"animate-pulse h-16 bg-gray-200 rounded-lg"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stock&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;150&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toFixed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;change&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toFixed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StockTicker&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;change&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;change&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;showMarketChart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Show a historical market chart for a stock symbol'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;parameters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;describe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'The stock ticker symbol'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;enum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'1D'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'1W'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'1M'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'1Y'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;describe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'The historical timeframe'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;generate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;yield&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"animate-pulse h-48 bg-gray-200 rounded-lg"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Generating&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;interactive&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;chart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dataPoints&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Point ${i + 1}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;150&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MarketChart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dataPoints&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toDataStreamResponse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Next, on the client side, we use the hooks to submit messages and render the dynamic components directly within the chat timeline:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/page.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'use client'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useActions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useUIState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ai/rsc'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChatPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setInput&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;conversation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setConversation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useUIState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;submitUserMessage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useActions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

